\begin{abstract}
We present a complete and exact treatment of a \emph{modified Wiener attack} on RSA that
exploits both a small private exponent and partial knowledge of the most significant bits
of the prime sum $p+q$. For an RSA modulus $n = pq$ with primes $p > q$, public exponent
$e$, and private exponent $d$ satisfying the key equation $ed = k\varphi(n) + 1$, the
classical Wiener attack recovers $d$ when $d < n^{1/4}$. We sharpen the attack by
introducing a \emph{corrected modulus} $\nt = n + 1 - s$ built from an estimate $s$ of
$p+q$, so that the relevant approximation error is governed by the \emph{estimation
residual} $(p+q) - s$ rather than by $p+q$ itself. When a $\delta$-fraction of the most
significant bits of $p+q$ is known, the residual is bounded by some $\Delta$, and we show
that the convergent criterion holds---and hence $d$ is recovered---under the
\emph{partial-knowledge smallness condition} $2d(k\Delta + 1) < \nt$, which admits private
exponents up to roughly $n^{(1+\delta)/2}$. The contribution has three pillars, all exact
identities or sharp inequalities: an arithmetic engine reducing the key equation to an
exact rational approximation below the Legendre threshold; a recovery layer using Farey
separation to prove uniqueness and recover the exact exponent; and a factorization layer
showing that recovering $d$ is equivalent to factoring $n$ via the quadratic formula, whose
discriminant $(p+q)^2 - 4n = (p-q)^2$ is a perfect square. We chain all three into a single
end-to-end statement. Every result has been formally verified.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The RSA cryptosystem derives its security from the presumed hardness of integer
factorization. The public key is $(n, e)$ and the private key is $d$, where
$ed \equiv 1 \pmod{\varphi(n)}$ with $\varphi(n) = (p-1)(q-1)$, equivalently
$ed = k\varphi(n) + 1$ for some integer $k \ge 1$. Decryption cost scales with $d$,
creating an incentive to choose $d$ small. Wiener (1990) showed that if
$d < \tfrac{1}{3} n^{1/4}$, then $k/d$ is a continued-fraction convergent of $e/n$, and $d$
---hence the factorization---is recoverable in polynomial time.

We study a strengthening in which the attacker knows the most significant bits of the
\emph{prime sum} $p+q$. Because $n - \varphi(n) = (p+q) - 1$, the quantity $p+q$ is exactly
the obstruction separating the public $n$ from the secret $\varphi(n)$; estimating it
shrinks that obstruction and sharpens the approximation. Our treatment is \emph{exact}
(every reduction is an algebraic identity, every bound a sharp inequality with explicit
hypotheses) and \emph{complete} (we prove the final factorization step and the structural
fact that makes it exact, then chain everything into a capstone). The parenthesized names
are the corresponding formally verified theorem names.

\section{Preliminaries and Definitions}

Throughout, $p, q, e, d, k, s, \Delta, a, b$ are integers, with $p > q$ the RSA primes,
$n = pq$, $e$ the public exponent, $d$ the private exponent, and $k$ the cofactor.

\begin{definition}[Totient of a semiprime; \texttt{phiSemiprime}]
For integers $p, q$ define $\varphi(p, q) := (p - 1)(q - 1)$. For $n = pq$ this is Euler's
totient $\varphi(n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Corrected modulus; \texttt{correctedModulus}]
Given an estimate $s$ of $p+q$, define $\nt(p, q, s) := pq + 1 - s$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Totient gap; \texttt{n\_sub\_phi}]
For all integers $p, q$, \; $pq - \varphi(p,q) = (p + q) - 1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Expand $\varphi(p,q) = pq - p - q + 1$ and subtract from $pq$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Perfect estimate recovers the totient; \texttt{correctedModulus\_perfect}]
For all integers $p, q$, \; $\nt(p, q, \, p+q) = \varphi(p, q)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\nt(p,q,p+q) = pq + 1 - (p+q) = (p-1)(q-1) = \varphi(p,q)$.
\end{proof}

A perfect estimate of $p+q$ turns the corrected modulus into the totient, where the Wiener
approximation is sharpest; real attacks use an imperfect $s$, and the residual $(p+q)-s$
controls the degradation.

\section{The Arithmetic Engine}

\begin{theorem}[Classical key identity; \texttt{rsa\_key\_identity}]
If $ed = k\,\varphi(p,q) + 1$, then $ed - k(pq) = 1 - k\bigl((p+q) - 1\bigr).$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitute $\varphi(p,q) = (p-1)(q-1)$ into the hypothesis and expand; the residual
$ed - kpq$ collapses to $1 - k((p+q)-1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Modified key identity; \texttt{modified\_key\_identity}]
If $ed = k\,\varphi(p,q) + 1$, then for any estimate $s$,
$ed - k\,\nt(p,q,s) = 1 - k\bigl((p+q) - s\bigr).$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
As above, with $\nt = pq + 1 - s$; the residual is now controlled by the estimation error
$(p+q) - s$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact approximation error; \texttt{modified\_approx\_error}]
Over $\Q$, if $ed = k\varphi(p,q) + 1$, $\nt \ne 0$, and $d \ne 0$, then
\[
\frac{e}{\nt} - \frac{k}{d} = \frac{1 - k\bigl((p+q) - s\bigr)}{\nt \cdot d}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Cast the modified key identity to $\Q$; clear denominators in the target identity (valid
since $\nt, d \ne 0$) and substitute.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Approximation bound; \texttt{modified\_approx\_abs\_bound}]
If $ed = k\varphi(p,q) + 1$, $|(p+q) - s| \le \Delta$, $k \ge 0$, $\nt > 0$, and $d > 0$,
then
\[
\left|\frac{e}{\nt} - \frac{k}{d}\right| \le \frac{k\Delta + 1}{\nt \cdot d}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The left side equals $|1 - k((p+q)-s)|/(\nt d)$. Bound the numerator:
$|1 - k((p+q)-s)| \le 1 + k|(p+q)-s| \le 1 + k\Delta$, using $k \ge 0$ and
$|(p+q)-s| \le \Delta$. Positivity of $\nt, d$ preserves the inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Modified Wiener convergent criterion; \texttt{modified\_wiener\_convergent\_criterion}]
Under the previous hypotheses together with the partial-knowledge smallness condition
$2d(k\Delta + 1) < \nt$, we have
\[
\left|\frac{e}{\nt} - \frac{k}{d}\right| < \frac{1}{2 d^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The left side is at most $(k\Delta+1)/(\nt d)$. It suffices that this be $< 1/(2d^2)$, i.e.
$2d^2(k\Delta+1) < \nt d$, i.e. (dividing by $d > 0$) $2d(k\Delta+1) < \nt$, the smallness
condition.
\end{proof}

By Legendre's theorem, a fraction within $1/(2d^2)$ of $x$ is a continued-fraction
convergent of $x$; thus the secret fraction $k/d$ appears among the convergents of the
public $e/\nt$. With no bits known, $\Delta \approx \sqrt n$ recovers Wiener's
$d \lesssim n^{1/4}$; knowing a $\delta$-fraction gives $\Delta \approx n^{(1-\delta)/2}$
and admits $d \lesssim n^{(1+\delta)/2}$.

\section{Recovery: Uniqueness of the Convergent}

\begin{theorem}[Farey separation; \texttt{farey\_separation}]
For integers with $b > 0$ and $e > 0$, if $a/b \ne c/e$ (i.e. $ae \ne cb$), then
$\left|\frac{a}{b} - \frac{c}{e}\right| \ge \frac{1}{b \cdot e}.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$a/b - c/e = (ae - cb)/(be)$; since $ae \ne cb$ are integers, $|ae - cb| \ge 1$. Divide by
$be > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of recovery; \texttt{wiener\_unique\_recovery}]
Let $x \in \Q$ and $0 < b \le d$, $0 < d$. If $|x - k/d| < 1/(2d^2)$ and
$|x - a/b| < 1/(2d^2)$, then $k/d = a/b$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If not, the triangle inequality gives $|k/d - a/b| < 1/(2d^2) + 1/(2d^2) = 1/d^2$, while
Farey separation gives $|k/d - a/b| \ge 1/(db) \ge 1/d^2$ (using $b \le d$); contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Denominator recovery under coprimality; \texttt{wiener\_recovery\_eq\_of\_coprime}]
Under the previous hypotheses, if additionally $\gcd(k, d) = 1$, then $b = d$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
From $k/d = a/b$, cross-multiplying gives $kb = ad$, so $d \mid kb$; since $\gcd(k,d) = 1$,
$d \mid b$, and with $0 < b \le d$ this forces $b = d$.
\end{proof}

Only the true fraction $k/d$ need be in lowest terms; coprimality of the candidate $a/b$ is
unnecessary.

\section{Factorization: The Missing Final Step}

\begin{theorem}[Perfect-square discriminant; \texttt{discriminant\_eq}]
For all reals $p, q$, \; $(p + q)^2 - 4(pq) = (p - q)^2.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$(p+q)^2 - 4pq = p^2 - 2pq + q^2 = (p-q)^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Closed-form factorization; \texttt{factor\_from\_sum\_prod}]
For reals $p > q$,
\[
p = \frac{(p+q) + \sqrt{(p+q)^2 - 4pq}}{2}, \qquad
q = \frac{(p+q) - \sqrt{(p+q)^2 - 4pq}}{2}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The radicand is $(p-q)^2$, so its square root is $|p-q| = p-q$ since $p > q$. Substitute.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Factoring $n$ from the totient; \texttt{factor\_n\_from\_totient}]
For integers $p > q$, set $S := pq - \varphi(p,q) + 1$. Then $S = p+q$, and
\[
p = \frac{S + \sqrt{S^2 - 4(pq)}}{2}, \qquad q = \frac{S - \sqrt{S^2 - 4(pq)}}{2}
\]
as real numbers.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$pq - \varphi(p,q) = (p+q) - 1$, so $S = p+q$; cast to $\R$ and apply the closed-form
factorization with sum $S$ and product $pq$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Totient from the key; \texttt{totient\_from\_key}]
If $ed = k\,\varphi(p,q) + 1$, then $k \cdot \varphi(p,q) = ed - 1.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Rearrange the key equation.
\end{proof}

With $k \ne 0$, this yields $\varphi(n) = (ed-1)/k$. Knowing $(k,d)$ determines $\varphi(n)$,
hence $p+q$, hence the primes; conversely knowing $p,q$ gives $\varphi(n)$ and thus $d$.
Recovery and factorization are two faces of one fact, mediated by the perfect square
$(p+q)^2 - 4n = (p-q)^2$.

\section{The End-to-End Theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Modified Wiener, end-to-end; \texttt{modified\_wiener\_end\_to\_end}]
Let $p > q$ be integers and suppose:
(1) $ed = k\,\varphi(p,q) + 1$;
(2) $|(p+q) - s| \le \Delta$;
(3) $k \ge 0$, $\nt(p,q,s) > 0$, $d > 0$;
(4) $2d(k\Delta + 1) < \nt(p,q,s)$;
(5) $\gcd(k, d) = 1$;
(6) $0 < b \le d$ and $\left|\frac{e}{\nt(p,q,s)} - \frac{a}{b}\right| < \frac{1}{2d^2}$.
Then $b = d$, and
\[
p = \frac{S + \sqrt{S^2 - 4n}}{2}, \qquad S = n - \varphi(n) + 1, \quad n = pq.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The smallness condition (4) feeds the convergent criterion to give
$|e/\nt - k/d| < 1/(2d^2)$. With (6) and $0 < b \le d$, denominator recovery (using (5))
forces $b = d$. The closed form for $p$ is the totient-factorization theorem.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithm}

\noindent\textbf{Modified Wiener Factorization.}
\emph{Input:} public key $(n,e)$; estimate $s$ of $p+q$ with residual bound $\Delta$.
\emph{Output:} primes $p,q$ or failure.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Form $\nt \leftarrow n + 1 - s$.
  \item Compute the continued-fraction convergents $a_i/b_i$ of $e/\nt$.
  \item For each convergent $a/b$ with $b > 0$: set $k \leftarrow a$, $d \leftarrow b$;
  if $k \mid (ed-1)$ set $\varphi' \leftarrow (ed-1)/k$, $S \leftarrow n - \varphi' + 1$,
  $D \leftarrow S^2 - 4n$; if $D \ge 0$ is a perfect square with root $t$, output
  $p \leftarrow (S+t)/2$, $q \leftarrow (S-t)/2$ if $pq = n$.
  \item Otherwise report failure.
\end{enumerate}
Correctness follows from the convergent criterion (true $k/d$ is enumerated), denominator
recovery (the test selects $d$), and the factorization theorems (the perfect-square test is
the integral form of $D = (p-q)^2$). The attack runs in time polynomial in $\log n$.

\section{Worked Example}

Let $p = 17$, $q = 11$, so $n = 187$, $\varphi(n) = 160$, $e = 7$, $d = 23$, $k = 1$. With a
perfect estimate $s = 28$, $\nt = 160$. Then
\[
\frac{7}{160} - \frac{1}{23} = \frac{1}{3680} < \frac{1}{1058} = \frac{1}{2\cdot 23^2}
\quad(\texttt{worked\_example\_below\_threshold}),
\]
and the Farey bound is attained with equality,
$|1/23 - 7/160| = 1/3680 = 1/(23\cdot 160)$ (\texttt{worked\_example\_separation}). Finally
$S = 28$, discriminant $28^2 - 4\cdot 187 = 36 = 6^2$, so $p = (28+6)/2 = 17$,
$q = (28-6)/2 = 11$ (\texttt{worked\_example\_factor}); $187$ is factored.

\section{Applications, Discussion, and Future Work}

The smallness condition gives implementers a precise, non-asymptotic safety margin relating
leaked MSBs of $p+q$ to the largest safely usable private exponent; it also models high-order
side-channel leakage directly. Future directions include: a quantitative
$\delta$-bound $d < n^{(1+\delta)/2}$ (a real-analysis estimate on top of the finite
criterion); a formal proof that recovery and factorization are exactly equivalent over $\Z$
via integer square roots; robustness bounds quantifying how recovered-prime error degrades
linearly with $\Delta$; and generalization to unbalanced and multi-prime moduli, where
symmetric functions replace $p+q$ and the discriminant structure generalizes to cubic
resolvents.

\section{Conclusion}

We gave a complete, exact account of a modified Wiener attack exploiting a small private
exponent and partial knowledge of $p+q$. The arithmetic engine reduces the key equation to a
sharp rational approximation; the recovery layer uses Farey separation to pin down the unique
convergent and the exact private exponent; and the factorization layer closes the loop with
the quadratic formula and its perfect-square discriminant. The end-to-end theorem shows that,
under the partial-knowledge smallness condition, the attack recovers $d$ and factors $n$ in
closed form---the two being one and the same fact.

